Here is what each term in that result means.

Colours

RED: needs urgent action.
AMBER: needs watching or a normal follow-up.
GREEN: no problem found.
GREY: the reading looks wrong, so the child is sent for a recheck instead of getting a colour.

People in the tests

T0001 to T0005: five fake test children.
Child A (C0001): the main demo child, a 14-month-old girl.
Mother of Child A (M0001): a lactating (breastfeeding) mother.
W07: the health worker assigned to Child A.

Rule codes

C1: weight is very low for height. A child more than 3 SD below normal is RED.
C2: MUAC (the arm band measurement) is below 115 mm. RED.
C7: the child hasn’t gained weight for 2 weeks in a row. AMBER.
C8: the last measurement is more than 35 days old. AMBER.
C10: a referral was issued but the child didn’t attend within 7 days. RED.
M3: a breastfeeding mother’s haemoglobin is below 8. RED.
M5: a pregnant mother’s height is below 140 cm. AMBER.
M6: a pregnant mother’s monthly weight gain is outside 1 to 3 kg. AMBER.

Health words

SD: how far a child is from the normal average. -3 SD is very far below.
MUAC: mid-upper arm circumference, a quick check for malnutrition.
Oedema: swelling of both feet, a sign of severe malnutrition. RED.
Danger sign: a warning sign the worker ticks, such as the child being unable to drink. RED.
Haemoglobin (Hb): the blood iron level. Low means anaemia.
BMI: weight relative to height. Below 18.5 is AMBER, below 16 is RED.
Trimester: the stage of pregnancy.
Referral: sending the child to a health centre.

Data terms

Decimal slip: a typing mistake such as 105 kg instead of 10.5 kg. The check suggests the fix.
Swapped: weight and height entered in each other’s boxes.
Future date: a measurement date that hasn’t happened yet.
Missing MUAC: the reading wasn’t taken, so the colour is shown with lower confidence.
Recheck: measure again. If the new reading is believable, GREY becomes a real colour.
Trust summary: a count of how many readings are believable and which mistakes are most common.



